  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1126 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nAfter a very warm October day, a calm night is on display across\nthe Mid-South with clear skies and temperatures in the 60s and\n70s. With clear skies and calm winds along a very shallow low-\nlevel inversion, low-lying areas mainly along the Tennessee River\nmay see some brief patchy fog overnight. Dense fog is not\nexpected as dry air continues to filter in across the area. Well\nabove temperature normal days are ahead as upper-level ridging\ncontinues to broaden over the Lower Mississippi Valley from the\nwestern Gulf. We'll be flirting with potential record breaking\nwarmth through at least Thursday with highs largely in the low\n90s. Dry conditions will persist through Tuesday as little to no\nsurface nor upper-level support will be present for any increased\nprecipitation chances.\n\nOne thing of note, southerly winds will begin to usher in Gulf\nmoisture late Monday into Tuesday increasing our dewpoints into\nthe mid 60s increasing humidity and leading to more warm and\nhumid conditions as opposed to the warm and dry conditions we\nfelt last week. Come Wednesday, shower and thunderstorm chances\nwill begin to increase in areas mainly north of the I-40 corridor\nas a surface front stalls just north of our area. We're not\nanticipating anything severe as instability looks to remain north\nof the Mid-South with the latest LREF guidance hinting at <20%\nchance of SBCAPE >500 J/kg and 0-6 bulk wind shear >25 kts in our\narea. As far as rainfall amounts go, latest NBM probs have a 30-\n50% chance of exceeding 1/2 an inch in the aforementioned areas.\nThough these amounts are nothing to write home about, we'll\nappreciate any rainfall we can get as many areas north of I-\n40 are currently under severe (D2) to extreme (D3) drought\nconditions. Precipitation chances begin to taper off by Thursday\nmorning as we lose our instability.\n\nLate Thursday into early Friday, a pattern change begins. Though\nuncertainty remains with the overall evolution of the upper and\nsurface level pattern, ensembles are becoming more aligned with a\ncooler solution. An upper-level trough looks to begin digging\ninto the Midwest from the Rockies with an attendant cold front\npushing across the Mid-South. Our next shot at true Fall-like\ntemperatures with highs in the 60s and 70s looks likely next\nweekend as the airmass behind the aforementioned front looks\nalmost polar in nature. We could even see some lows in the upper\n30s to lower 40s come Sunday morning, however, for this, we will\nhave to wait and see.\n\nAEH",
